REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w12","title":"Professional writing: paragraphs, formal style, editing and proofreading","summary":"The last week is about writing at work. It covers what makes writing professional, how to build a paragraph (topic, supporting and concluding sentences, unity and coherence), how to keep the style formal and the tone objective, how to edit and proofread (with the 14 most common surface errors), and how to write well in an exam.","n":12,"lectures":[{"code":"12.1","title":"Professional writing | definition, elements & clarity in formal communication","video":"mzIYctZ7HGg","seconds":884},{"code":"12.2","title":"Development & organization of paragraphs","video":"IXbFjs4HHXI","seconds":2264},{"code":"12.3","title":"Professional writing (language & style: aspects of formality, tone in formal writing)","video":"ex0qeyRwv-g","seconds":2014},{"code":"12.4","title":"Professional writing: editing & proofreading","video":"po55EcHnXbY","seconds":2368},{"code":"12.5","title":"Improve your writing skills","video":"SP97dPNQmLM","seconds":488}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-professional-writing-is","level":2,"title":"What professional writing is"},{"id":"professional-means-formal","level":3,"title":"Professional means formal"},{"id":"professional-writing-and-literary-writing","level":3,"title":"Professional writing and literary writing"},{"id":"the-four-elements-of-professional-writing","level":3,"title":"The four elements of professional writing"},{"id":"building-a-paragraph","level":2,"title":"Building a paragraph"},{"id":"the-three-parts-of-a-paragraph","level":3,"title":"The three parts of a paragraph"},{"id":"the-topic-sentence","level":3,"title":"The topic sentence"},{"id":"supporting-sentences","level":3,"title":"Supporting sentences"},{"id":"the-concluding-sentence","level":3,"title":"The concluding sentence"},{"id":"unity-and-coherence","level":3,"title":"Unity and coherence"},{"id":"strategies-for-unification","level":3,"title":"Strategies for unification"},{"id":"strategies-for-making-an-argument","level":3,"title":"Strategies for making an argument"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-example-paragraph","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s example paragraph"},{"id":"formal-style-and-clarity","level":2,"title":"Formal style and clarity"},{"id":"formal-and-informal-style","level":3,"title":"Formal and informal style"},{"id":"what-makes-language-formal","level":3,"title":"What makes language formal"},{"id":"what-makes-language-informal","level":3,"title":"What makes language informal"},{"id":"clarity-no-wordy-cliche-d-or-jargonised-phrases","level":3,"title":"Clarity: no wordy, clichéd or jargonised phrases"},{"id":"how-to-avoid-cliche-s-and-jargon","level":3,"title":"How to avoid clichés and jargon"},{"id":"objective-tone","level":2,"title":"Objective tone"},{"id":"editing","level":2,"title":"Editing"},{"id":"editing-for-content","level":3,"title":"Editing for content"},{"id":"editing-for-overall-structure","level":3,"title":"Editing for overall structure"},{"id":"editing-for-clarity","level":3,"title":"Editing for clarity"},{"id":"editing-for-style","level":3,"title":"Editing for style"},{"id":"proofreading","level":2,"title":"Proofreading"},{"id":"four-proofreading-techniques","level":3,"title":"Four proofreading techniques"},{"id":"what-proofreading-checks","level":3,"title":"What proofreading checks"},{"id":"the-fourteen-most-common-surface-errors","level":3,"title":"The fourteen most common surface errors"},{"id":"writing-for-an-exam","level":2,"title":"Writing for an exam"},{"id":"keep-it-simple","level":3,"title":"Keep it simple"},{"id":"set-phrases-and-linking-words","level":3,"title":"Set phrases and linking words"},{"id":"formal-and-short","level":3,"title":"Formal and short"},{"id":"read-check-and-get-it-checked","level":3,"title":"Read, check, and get it checked"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Professional writing</strong> (12.1): it gives <strong>authentic</strong> information by <strong>systematic rules</strong>, to a <strong>specific audience</strong>, for a <strong>purpose</strong>. Examples: product catalogues, brochures, the medical description on a tablet strip, user manuals, workplace reports and emails. Unlike <strong>literary</strong> writing, it is written so that readers can <strong>act</strong>: it informs and instructs in <strong>direct, factual, specific</strong> language. Its four <strong>elements</strong>: organised paragraphs, an appropriate (formal) style, clarity of ideas, and spelling and grammar.</li>\n<li><strong>Paragraphs</strong> (12.2): a <strong>topic sentence</strong> (an arguable claim; first or second in professional writing), then <strong>supporting sentences</strong> (reasons, data, quotations, examples), then a <strong>concluding sentence</strong> (required in a stand-alone paragraph). <strong>Unity</strong> means one idea per paragraph; <strong>coherence</strong> means a logical flow. Six ways to build an argument: an example, a story, compare and contrast, cause and effect, data, a definition. Real paragraphs <strong>embed</strong> several of them.</li>\n<li><strong>Formal style and clarity</strong> (12.3): formal style is <strong>less personal</strong> (not impersonal), used for professional purposes, with an <strong>objective</strong> tone. Avoid <strong>group jargon</strong>, <strong>slang and idioms</strong>, <strong>biased language</strong>, <strong><em>I</em>, <em>we</em> and <em>you</em></strong>, and <strong>contractions</strong>. For clarity, also avoid <strong>wordy phrases</strong>, <strong>clichés</strong> and <strong>jargon</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Objective tone</strong> (12.3): write about ideas, not people. Make no judgement without evidence, and no <em>terrible</em> or <em>wonderful</em>. Use <strong>modality</strong> (<em>there is a possibility</em>) to leave room for disagreement, and cite <strong>authentic sources</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Editing</strong> (12.4): revise the organisation of the draft: <strong>content</strong>, <strong>overall structure</strong>, <strong>clarity</strong> and <strong>style</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Proofreading</strong> (12.4): correct the surface. Read aloud, check your own list of errors first, look for <strong>one error at a time</strong>, and check the format. Then check the <strong>14 common surface errors</strong>, from the missing comma after an introductory phrase to the misplaced modifier.</li>\n<li><strong>Exam writing</strong> (12.5): <strong>keep it simple</strong> and accurate. Prepare set phrases and <strong>linking words</strong>, use no contractions, and cut <strong>deadweight</strong> words. Read the kinds of writing the exam asks for, <strong>write less and check more</strong>, and get your work checked.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-professional-writing-is\">What professional writing is</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.1 opens the course’s last week. Weeks 10 and 11 practised many kinds of writing: bio-notes, describing a photo, notes, business letters, essays. This week asks what makes writing <strong>professional</strong>, and how you produce it. Lectures 12.1 to 12.4 follow one plan: 12.1 defines professional writing and names its four <strong>elements</strong>, and the next three lectures take the elements one by one (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=20s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:20</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). Lecture 12.5 adds tips for writing exams.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Professional writing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=40s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:40</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“A style of writing used in delivering <strong>authentic</strong> information on a subject by following <strong>systematic rules</strong>.” (<em>Authentic</em> = true and reliable. <em>Systematic</em> = done by a fixed method.)</li>\n<li>“The intended <strong>audience</strong> is specific and needs to possess recommended levels of knowledge on the subject in order to understand the content.” The writer can expect the readers to know the subject up to a certain level.</li>\n<li>“The writing has a <strong>purpose</strong> to serve.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecturer names its properties: a <strong>formal style</strong>, a <strong>specific audience</strong> and its <strong>language style</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"professional-means-formal\">Professional means formal</h3>\n<p>To understand professional writing, first understand <strong>professional</strong> and <strong>professionalism</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). Their main feature is the <strong>formal</strong> nature of the <em>domain</em> (an area of work or life). “It is not like how you converse with your friends.” The domain is clearly mapped out with rules, procedures and guidelines, and all of them apply to professional writing too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n<p>Who decides that the style must be formal? <strong>The audience</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). At work, your readers are people in an office, in a classroom, or anywhere the reader is “not already known to you” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Where you meet professional writing (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Most of it is in the business world:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>catalogues of products;</li>\n<li>brochures (<em>brochure</em> = a small booklet about a product or a service);</li>\n<li>the medical description on a tablet strip;</li>\n<li>user manuals;</li>\n<li>a report in a company, and emails at a workplace (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> “anything can come under the ambit of professional writing” (<em>ambit</em> = range), if it follows the rules: formal language, a target audience, authentic information and a formal style (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"professional-writing-and-literary-writing\">Professional writing and literary writing</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:50</a>) puts the two side by side:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Professional writing</th>\n<th>Literary writing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What it does</td>\n<td>“manages specific information so as to take suitable actions”</td>\n<td>creative constructions: poems, novels and other compilations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Written to</td>\n<td><strong>inform</strong> and <strong>instruct</strong></td>\n<td><strong>entertain</strong> and <strong>amuse</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Language</td>\n<td><strong>direct</strong>, <strong>factual</strong>, <strong>specific</strong> and <strong>straightforward</strong></td>\n<td>techniques such as <strong>hyperbole</strong> and <strong>metaphor</strong>, for imagination and creativity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Words and sentences</td>\n<td><strong>technical vocabulary</strong>, <strong>simple</strong> sentences</td>\n<td><strong>complex</strong> sentence structures; ambiguity, dialects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tone</td>\n<td><strong>impersonal</strong> and <strong>objective</strong></td>\n<td>appeals to the <strong>emotions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Readers</td>\n<td>a <strong>specific</strong> audience</td>\n<td><strong>general</strong> readers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>What each row means:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Action.</strong> The key difference is in the first row: professional writing is written “to take suitable action” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). Literary writing has other purposes. Besides informing and instructing, professional writing may also <strong>persuade</strong> or <strong>describe</strong>. Literature is mostly about entertainment and amusement (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Figures of speech.</strong> Literary language uses <strong>figures of speech</strong>: the metaphorical or figurative use of language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>). A <em>hyperbole</em> is a deliberate exaggeration (<em>I have told you a million times</em>). A <em>metaphor</em> calls one thing another to compare them (<em>Time is money</em>). (These two examples are mine.)</li>\n<li><strong>Technical vocabulary.</strong> At work the audience is specific, so the words are specialised. They “may not be something that is accessible to everybody, to a layman” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). (<em>Layman</em> = a person with no special knowledge of the subject.)</li>\n<li><strong>Creative liberty.</strong> A novel or a play may use <strong>dialects</strong> (the local form of a language, spoken in one region or by one group). It may also use “improper” language: language that does not follow the structure of English, or that some people find inappropriate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). Professional writing has no such freedom: “There is a set guideline” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Readers.</strong> Literature has genres for particular readers: adult fiction, young adult fiction, writing about the <em>diaspora</em> (people who live far from their home country), about one period of history, or about philosophical change (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). Still, “there is no restriction” on who can read it. A company’s manual or report, or an email at a workplace, “is not meant for everybody” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Simple sentences” here, “complex sentences” in 12.2</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide gives professional writing <strong>simple sentences</strong>, as against the long, layered and ambiguous sentences of literature. Lecture 12.2 recommends <strong>complex sentences</strong> for topic sentences, where <em>complex</em> is a grammar term: one main clause plus a dependent clause. The two do not clash: a clear complex sentence is still plain, direct writing. (This note is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-four-elements-of-professional-writing\">The four elements of professional writing</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=580s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:40</a>) lists four elements:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Developed and organised paragraphs</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). All writing needs them, “but more so” professional writing. <em>Organisation</em> is “the logic of writing itself”: what goes first, what goes in the middle, what goes at the end, and where to place each part for the effect you want (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>). <em>Development</em> comes before organisation, and the <strong>thought process</strong> comes before both. First your ideas are in place; then you develop them into a structure the reader can follow (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Appropriate writing style</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). Here this means the <strong>formal</strong> style. Four things make writing formal: the structure or <strong>template</strong> you use, the <strong>audience</strong>, the <strong>medium</strong> (the channel: an email, a letter, a report), and “above everything” the way you use the language, the <strong>register</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). (<em>Register</em> = the level of formality of the language you choose for a situation. This definition is mine.) The language should be direct, factual and straightforward (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Clarity of ideas</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). This is mostly about language: the guidelines that give your writing clear thoughts and clear ideas.</li>\n<li><strong>Spelling and grammar</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>). It is “the most ignored part” of most writing. Writing full of spelling and grammar errors “would not be taken seriously by anybody who reads it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzIYctZ7HGg&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). It usually comes at the end, when you <strong>proofread</strong> your work.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Where each element returns</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Lecture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Developed and organised paragraphs</td>\n<td>12.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Formal style, clarity and tone</td>\n<td>12.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spelling and grammar, with the whole draft checked</td>\n<td>12.4 (editing and proofreading)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"building-a-paragraph\">Building a paragraph</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.2 takes the first element. A <strong>paragraph</strong> is a group of sentences about one idea. The lecture has two parts: the <strong>structure</strong> of a paragraph and the <strong>strategies</strong> for writing a good one (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=10s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:10</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). The techniques work for professional writing and for general writing, “to bring more structure and impact to what you are writing”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-parts-of-a-paragraph\">The three parts of a paragraph</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The structure of a paragraph</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s diagram (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>) stacks three layers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Topic sentence</strong>: it states the topic and the main point. It is “a mandatory element in all kinds of paragraphs” (<em>mandatory</em> = required) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting sentences</strong>: they add to, prove or explain the main point.</li>\n<li><strong>Concluding sentence</strong>: it closes the paragraph. “Sometimes it is just one sentence”; sometimes there are more (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Where the topic sentence goes</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The order need not be “linear” (first topic, then support, then conclusion). In general writing the topic sentence can come at the beginning, at the end or in the middle (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). <strong>In professional writing</strong>, put the topic sentence <strong>first or second</strong>, “to avoid any forms of confusions or ambiguities”. (<em>Ambiguity</em> = a meaning that can be read in more than one way.) Later the lecture says it more strongly: it is “strongly recommended that your topic sentence always is used as the first sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-topic-sentence\">The topic sentence</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Topic sentence: what it is</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:20</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“The topic sentence makes a <strong>claim</strong> about the topic in question.” A <em>claim</em> is a statement that you say is true and that you must then show to be true. It is “essentially an argument” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li>“It should be <strong>arguable</strong> i.e. the paragraph should be such that the claim is established through it.” It “should not be a statement of fact”: the supporting sentences must be able to prove it or add to it.</li>\n<li>“It should reveal the main point / <strong>purpose</strong> of the paragraph” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>): the central theme of what the author wants to write.</li>\n<li>“It should also connect to the overall attitude of the paragraph.” It does not sit apart: it must “tie up well” with the supporting and concluding sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A fact or a claim? (my sentences)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Water boils at 100 °C at sea level.</em> This is a fact. Nobody argues with it, so there is nothing left for the paragraph to prove. It is not a good topic sentence.</li>\n<li><em>Every hostel room should have a water purifier.</em> This is a claim. A reader may disagree, so the supporting sentences have work to do: reasons, facts, examples. It is a good topic sentence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> a topic sentence opens a question, and the rest of the paragraph answers it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>How do you write a topic sentence? The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>) shows three forms: <strong>complex sentences</strong>, <strong>questions</strong> and the <strong>pivot</strong>. The first needs two grammar words.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Clauses and the complex sentence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>clause</strong> is a group of words with its own subject and verb.</li>\n<li>An <strong>independent clause</strong> (a main clause) makes complete sense alone: <em>the plan failed</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>dependent clause</strong> (a subordinate clause) does not: <em>because nobody tested it</em> (then what?).</li>\n<li>A <strong>complex sentence</strong> has at least one independent clause and at least one dependent clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>): <em>The plan failed because nobody tested it.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(The example sentences are mine. Clauses and conjunctions were in <a href=\"#/english1/w02/conjunctions\">Week 2</a>.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The three forms of a topic sentence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A complex sentence.</strong> Most real writing uses complex sentences: essays, research papers, articles in magazines and newspapers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>). They have “better scope to represent things in its entirety” than a simple sentence. So the lecture suggests a complex sentence for a topic sentence, although complex sentences can also be supporting or concluding sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). My example: <em>Although online classes save travel time, they demand more self-discipline than classroom learning.</em></li>\n<li><strong>A question.</strong> People are curious by nature. A question makes the reader think and want to read on (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). Use it above all for a topic that is new, intriguing or fascinating (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). My example: <em>Why do so many students stop reading for pleasure after school?</em></li>\n<li><strong>A pivot.</strong> A <em>pivot</em> is a centre point, a fulcrum: the point on which something turns (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>). Sometimes a writer wants to <strong>change direction</strong> in the middle of a paragraph. Then the topic sentence becomes the turning point, and it shows the turn with a <strong>transitional device</strong> such as <em>however</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). My example: <em>However, the new timetable has created problems of its own.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"supporting-sentences\">Supporting sentences</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Supporting sentences</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:20</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“They form the <strong>substantial</strong> segment of a paragraph”: the main body, the largest part. The lecturer calls them “the meaty layer” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n<li>“They are tasked to add / explain, prove or, elaborate the main point or the central theme of the paragraph.” (<em>Elaborate</em> = give more detail.) They usually follow the topic sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“These sentences could most commonly do the following”: (i) <strong>give reasons</strong>; (ii) <strong>cite facts / data / statistics</strong>; (iii) <strong>use quotations</strong>; (iv) <strong>give examples</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Which kind you use “depends on the topic that you have chosen and what you have decided to write about it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-concluding-sentence\">The concluding sentence</h3>\n<p>An essay has an <strong>introduction</strong>, a <strong>body</strong> and a <strong>conclusion</strong>. So it has an introduction paragraph, a conclusion paragraph, and <strong>body paragraphs</strong> between them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). This lecture is about body paragraphs; introduction and conclusion paragraphs “look a little different”.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Concluding sentence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“It is a <strong>mandatory</strong> segment in a <strong>stand-alone</strong> paragraph.” A <em>stand-alone</em> paragraph is a single paragraph that is the whole piece of writing.</li>\n<li>“The function of this sentence is to <strong>restate</strong> or <strong>reinforce</strong> the theme substantiated through the supporting sentences and the topic sentence.” (<em>Restate</em> = say again in other words. <em>Reinforce</em> = make stronger. <em>Substantiate</em> = support with evidence.)</li>\n<li>“A good concluding sentence could do the following”: (i) make a <strong>prediction</strong>; (ii) give <strong>suggestion(s)</strong>; (iii) give <strong>recommendation(s)</strong>; (iv) <strong>summarise</strong> the key points; (v) offer a <strong>final observation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Does every paragraph need one? It depends on where the paragraph stands (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The paragraph is …</th>\n<th>A concluding sentence is …</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>stand-alone (a single paragraph)</td>\n<td><strong>required</strong>. Without it the paragraph seems to end “abruptly”, gets no “closure”, and “looks very out of place” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a body paragraph in a longer essay or text</td>\n<td><strong>not essential</strong> for every body paragraph</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Five kinds of concluding sentence (my sentences)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Take the stand-alone paragraph that begins <em>Every hostel room should have a water purifier.</em> It could end in five ways:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Concluding sentence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Prediction</td>\n<td><em>With clean water in every room, fewer students will fall ill during exams.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Suggestion</td>\n<td><em>The hostel could start with one purifier on each floor.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommendation</td>\n<td><em>The hostel committee should approve purifiers for all rooms this year.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Summary</td>\n<td><em>Purifiers would protect health, save money on bottled water and cut plastic waste.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Final observation</td>\n<td><em>Clean water, in the end, is a basic need and not a luxury.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A suggestion offers an idea to consider; a recommendation is firmer advice about what should be done. (This difference is mine.) The lecture adds that a final observation usually closes a paragraph of opinions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"unity-and-coherence\">Unity and coherence</h3>\n<p>Two features make any good paragraph: <strong>unity</strong> and <strong>coherence</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). People treat them separately, “but then they act together”.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Unity</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>One idea per paragraph.</strong> Unity is “the maintenance of a single theme”. Do not bring “too many ideas, themes together” into one paragraph (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=850s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:10</a>): “Unity is a significant requirement in writing a paragraph. The components of a paragraph must present and point towards a single idea and provide for a wholesome idea.” Unity gives the reader <strong>clarity</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Coherence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The <strong>logical continuity</strong> that is required to write a paragraph” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>). The first, second and third sentences must be “connected to each other logically”; they must not look as if they come “from different domains” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). “Coherence basically means <strong>tied together</strong>.” You need it <strong>between sentences</strong> and, in a longer text, <strong>between paragraphs</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Unity and coherence in one line each</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Unity</strong> asks: <em>is every sentence about the same one idea?</em> <strong>Coherence</strong> asks: <em>does each sentence lead logically to the next?</em> The two overlap: “there is a good deal of overlap between unity and coherence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>). That is why the first strategy for unity, below, is logical continuity. (The two questions are mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"strategies-for-unification\">Strategies for unification</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=850s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:10</a>) gives three strategies to unify a paragraph:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ensure the logical continuity</strong> of the idea presented in the paragraph (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Repeat the key words.</strong> Each repetition reminds the reader: “this is the topic that we are talking about” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Use transitional words to add, contrast, or conclude</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). They give “good flow between the sentence[s]”, and they tie the whole idea “around one single point” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Transitional devices</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Words and phrases that link one sentence, or one paragraph, to the next. With them the reader moves on smoothly, without feeling a sudden break (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>). The lecture’s examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>To …</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Direction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>add</strong></td>\n<td><em>furthermore, moreover, additionally, in addition to</em></td>\n<td>the same direction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>contrast</strong></td>\n<td><em>nonetheless, nevertheless, despite, however, although, though</em></td>\n<td>a change of direction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>conclude</strong></td>\n<td><em>to sum up, to conclude, in conclusion</em></td>\n<td>the end</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"strategies-for-making-an-argument\">Strategies for making an argument</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>) gives six strategies. For a beginner they are “a good starting point”.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Use an example to further the argument</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>). You make a claim, give an example “that ties well with the argument”, and build on it.</li>\n<li><strong>Tell a story or narrate an anecdote, if you are writing a personal paragraph</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>). (<em>Anecdote</em> = a short story about a real event.) Use it for anything <strong>experiential</strong> (from your own experience): a travel blog, your experience with a gadget or an online purchase, a pilgrimage. Such <strong>narrative</strong> paragraphs use many <strong>personal pronouns</strong>: <em>I, you, we, they, he, she, it</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Compare and contrast</strong>, when the analysis must explore “the subtleties and nuances of related events / persons / period” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>). (<em>Subtle</em> = small and not obvious. <em>Nuance</em> = a very small difference in meaning or quality.)</li>\n<li><strong>Cause and effect</strong>: “useful when the relationship is that of one leading to the other” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Cite data, figures and statistics</strong> for “factual and scientific details”: when you keep away opinions and personal experience, and base everything on facts and science (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>A definition</strong>: it “suits when introducing a new concept”. You give the definition, then build your argument on it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Compare and contrast is not for obvious differences</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many people think that compare and contrast is about similarities and differences. That is only “partially right” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>). Do not compare a <strong>table</strong> and a <strong>chair</strong>: everybody knows how they differ. Compare <strong>two kinds of chairs</strong>, or two kinds of tables: their “intricate details” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>). The lecture’s other topics (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>): the Elizabethan and the Victorian ages of literature; World War One and World War Two; two dynasties that ruled the country. Compare and contrast is a method of <strong>analysis</strong>, used in analytical paragraphs.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Purpose decides</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Your purpose will then decide the medium, the style, the pattern.” Choose the strategy that “fits well, to the purpose” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Embedded patterns</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In real life a paragraph is rarely of only one type. Usually several patterns sit inside one paragraph: an <strong>embedded pattern</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>). (<em>Embed</em> = fix one thing firmly inside another.) For example, a compare-and-contrast paragraph can carry examples inside it, and a narrative paragraph about a personal experience can bring in an example or a comparison (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>). Longer texts, such as reflective essays and research papers, almost always combine the strategies (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-example-paragraph\">The lecture’s example paragraph</h3>\n<p>The lecture tests all this on one paragraph from Lien Devos’s essay on graphic novels (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:10</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Traditionally, Flemish comic-book culture was dominated by long-running comic series with an unchanging cast of characters. Most of those comics, initially devised as gag strips or series in newspapers and magazines, are now facing the same problems as the traditional print media in which they are published. For more than half a century, annual compilation albums of such comic strips were one of the main sources of entertainment for young people in Flanders, with print runs of more than 400,000 copies. Today, comics are part of a much wider entertainment context, competing with films, games, animation shorts, the internet, social media, and so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>(<em>Flemish</em> = from Flanders, the Dutch-speaking north of Belgium. A <em>gag strip</em> is a short comic strip that ends in a joke. A <em>compilation album</em> collects many strips in one book. A <em>print run</em> is the number of copies printed at one time. These glosses are mine.)</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the three parts (the lecture’s analysis at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sentence 1</strong> (<em>Traditionally, Flemish comic-book culture …</em>) is the <strong>topic sentence</strong>. It declares the topic: comic-book culture (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>). It comes first, as professional writing recommends (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Sentences 2 and 3</strong> (blue on the slide) are the <strong>supporting sentences</strong>. They add to what the topic sentence declares: the problems of print, and half a century of albums with print runs of more than 400,000 copies (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Sentence 4</strong> (<em>Today, comics are part of …</em>) is the <strong>closing</strong> sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>). It does not summarise. It adds a <strong>final observation</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Is it a concluding sentence? “Yes, it is.” Is it a traditional one? “No”, because the paragraph comes from a longer essay, where a body paragraph need not have a full concluding sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> topic sentence first, two supporting sentences, and a closing sentence that offers a final observation.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the strategies for unification (the lecture’s analysis at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Repeated key words</strong> (green on the slide): <em>comic-book culture, comic series, comics, comic strips</em>, and <em>comics</em> again in the last sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>). <em>Comic</em>, the key word of the topic sentence, comes back again and again “to reiterate to the reader” what the topic is (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Transitional devices</strong> (red on the slide): <em>Traditionally</em>, <em>For more than half a century</em>, <em>Today</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>). They are not the usual <em>add</em> or <em>contrast</em> words, but they are still important transitional devices: they build the link between sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=2100s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em><strong>Traditionally</strong></em> shows the author’s <strong>point of view</strong>, and it applies to the whole sentence that follows (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=2130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em><strong>For more than half a century</strong></em> is an <strong>introductory phrase</strong>. It continues the paragraph, adds to it and elaborates (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em><strong>Today</strong></em> gives a sense of <strong>time</strong>: the present (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the key word <em>comic</em> is repeated, and three transitional devices link the sentences. Transitional devices can appear anywhere in a paragraph; they act “as gel and glue between the sentences” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Space, time and direction</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Transitional devices arrange your ideas <strong>in space</strong>, <strong>in time</strong> (<em>traditionally</em>, <em>today</em>) and <strong>in a direction</strong>: the same way (<em>moreover</em>), a new way (<em>however</em>), or to the end (<em>in conclusion</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IXbFjs4HHXI&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">How to find the topic sentence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>From the lecture’s example: (1) it <strong>declares the topic</strong>; (2) its <strong>key word repeats</strong> through the paragraph; (3) in professional writing it comes <strong>first</strong> (or second). (This checklist is mine, built from the lecture.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"formal-style-and-clarity\">Formal style and clarity</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.3 takes the second and third elements: <strong>style</strong> and <strong>clarity</strong>. In professional writing “the style is always formal” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). The lecture has two parts: the <strong>aspects of formality</strong>, and the <strong>tone</strong> of formal writing (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=10s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:10</a>). It starts with the meaning of <em>formal</em> and <em>informal</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"formal-and-informal-style\">Formal and informal style</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=40s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:40</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Formal style</th>\n<th>Informal style</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Less personal</td>\n<td>1. More casual and spontaneous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Used for professional or academic purposes</td>\n<td>2. Used to write to friends and relatives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Uses an objective tone</td>\n<td>3. Uses an emotional tone</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<em>Spontaneous</em> = done without planning. <em>Objective</em> = based on facts, not on personal feelings.)</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Less personal”, not “impersonal”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide says that formal style is <strong>less personal</strong>. It does not say <em>impersonal</em>, or “completely depersonalized” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). Formality is “not black and white”: it is a <strong>range</strong>, from less formal to more formal, “and that is how real life situations work” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The email (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Email has become a main medium of professional communication, used by “all professionals from all fields” at their workplace (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</li>\n<li>An email is one-to-one or one-to-many communication: “mostly a form of personal communication” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n<li>So removing every personal element from an email is “an impossible task”.</li>\n<li>But it must not become so personal that it “sounds informal”, “indecent or impolite”, or unsuitable for work (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> an email is formal but <strong>less personal</strong>; it is not impersonal.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The other two rows of the table:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Purpose.</strong> Formal style is used when the purpose of the writing is “clearly defined” to the writer (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Tone.</strong> The <em>tone</em> is the attitude that a piece of writing shows. There are many tones: <em>ironic</em>, <em>emotional</em>, <em>intellectual</em>. Professional writing uses an <strong>objective</strong> tone (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). Informal writing uses an emotional tone, so professional writing “should not have emotional tones” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-makes-language-formal\">What makes language formal</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>) names three factors: <strong>specialised situations</strong>, <strong>audience expectations</strong> and <strong>purpose</strong>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Specialised situations</strong> means both the <strong>medium</strong> and the <strong>domain</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). The medium decides which point of the range of formality suits it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). The lecture’s examples, from less formal to more formal (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):\n<ul>\n<li>a text message to a friend;</li>\n<li>a text message to your boss;</li>\n<li>an email to the same boss;</li>\n<li>an application to a university, a job application form, a report to your authority.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Audience expectations</strong>: the audience expects different things from you, again depending on the medium (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Purpose</strong>: “Purpose is everything in professional writing”. It “has the power to decide everything else” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"what-makes-language-informal\">What makes language informal</h3>\n<p>To write well, it is not enough to know what is formal. You must also know “what should be avoided” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:40</a>) lists five factors.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">1. Group jargon</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Any <strong>in-group</strong> specialized language”: the words that only one group uses and understands (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>). The words change from group to group.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The festival words (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>IIT Madras students organise <strong>Shaastra</strong> and <strong>Saarang</strong>, the institute’s technical and cultural festivals.</li>\n<li>Among the organisers, <em>coords</em> means coordinators and <em>super coords</em> means super coordinators. There is also “an FR team” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li>An outsider who hears “please talk to the coord” or “the FR team” does not understand. That is not because of poor English: “this is a specialized language” of one group (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The rule:</strong> “In formal writing or in professional writing one should not use group jargon” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">2. Slang, idiomatic expressions, colloquialisms and local words</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s examples: <em>Y’all, Asap, cool, yo</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slang</strong>: very informal words, often used by one group or one age (<em>cool</em>, <em>yo</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Idiomatic expression</strong> (an idiom): a phrase whose meaning is not the meaning of its words (<em>break the ice</em>). Every language has its own idioms, and speakers of other languages may not follow them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Colloquialism</strong>: a word or phrase of everyday conversation (<em>y’all</em> = you all; <em>ASAP</em> = as soon as possible).</li>\n<li><strong>Local words</strong>: words so local that “even ... somebody from the next district” may not understand them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(The four definitions and <em>break the ice</em> are mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why avoid them? Professional writing “is written for a wider audience, it is documented”. These words can “shroud the meaning” (<em>shroud</em> = cover and hide) and create ambiguity (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">3. Stereotypical and biased language</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>stereotype</strong> is a fixed, over-simple idea about everyone in a group. <strong>Bias</strong> is an unfair leaning for or against a group. (These two definitions are mine.) The slide lists the categories that are “prone to” bias and stereotypes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>): <strong>gender, race, ethnicity, socio-economic status, age, disability, sexual orientation</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gender</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). Words such as <em>chairman</em>, <em>postman</em> and <em>mankind</em> are called <strong>sexist</strong>, “because it assumes that a person is always a man”. The slide adds <em>manpower</em> and <em>man-made</em>. Instead of <em>mankind</em>, the lecture suggests <em>human beings</em>, <em>individuals</em> or <em>persons</em>: these sound “more neutral”, and they are “more inclusive”.</li>\n<li><strong>Race and ethnicity</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>). Use the labels “formally recognized by both the community as well as the scholarship around that community”, such as <em>South Asians</em> or <em>African Americans</em>. The lecture adds that <em>Black</em> and <em>white</em> are also well recognised today.</li>\n<li><strong>Every category</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). Be “sensible and sensitive” to labels and to people’s identities. Do not use “false hierarchies” (do not rank groups as higher and lower), “stay away from stereotypes and avoid bias in language”.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Neutral words for gendered words (mine, after the lecture’s <em>mankind</em>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Instead of</th>\n<th>Write</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>mankind</em></td>\n<td><em>human beings, individuals, persons</em> (the lecture’s)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>chairman</em></td>\n<td><em>chairperson, chair</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>postman</em></td>\n<td><em>postal worker</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>manpower</em></td>\n<td><em>workforce, staff</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>man-made</em></td>\n<td><em>artificial, synthetic</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">4. First-person and second-person pronouns</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Do not use the <strong>first-person</strong> pronouns <em>I, me, my, we, us</em>. Do not use the <strong>second-person</strong> <em>you</em> either: “addressing your audiences you makes it thoroughly informal” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">5. Contractions</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>contraction</strong> is a short form that joins two words, with an apostrophe (') in place of the missing letters: <em>I’ve</em> = <em>I have</em>, <em>you’re</em> = <em>you are</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). “In formal style one should stay away from these contractions” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">You’re or your?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>You’re</em> (= you are) is not <em>your</em> (belonging to you) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). <em>You’re late</em> means <em>you are late</em>; <em>your report</em> is the report that belongs to you. (These two examples are mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">No “I” at all?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Formality is a range. A report or an application sits at the formal end: no <em>I</em>, no <em>you</em>, no contractions. An email is “less personal”, not impersonal, so some personal words remain. Even the lecture’s examples for other rules use <em>I</em> (<em>I realised that …</em>). (This note is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"clarity-no-wordy-cliche-d-or-jargonised-phrases\">Clarity: no wordy, clichéd or jargonised phrases</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:50</a>): “Formal writing avoids phrases or sentences that are wordy, clichéd, jargonised, and are indirect to ensure clarity.”</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Wordiness, clichés and jargon</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>“<strong>Wordiness</strong> includes lengthy sentences that owe their length to <strong>cluttered phrases</strong> that are irrelevant to the meaning of the sentence.” Such phrases “take up space” and add nothing to the meaning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“<strong>Clichés</strong> are overused expressions that lead to redundancy in writing.” Once they were popular; now they are so overused that they “sound redundant” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>). (<em>Redundant</em> = not needed.)</li>\n<li>“<strong>Jargons</strong> are inaccessible expressions that can make the writing ambiguous to the reader” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>). (<em>Inaccessible</em> = hard for most readers to understand.)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Wordy sentences (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:50</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>In my personal opinion, the project should be considered for approval.</em> Use <em>In my opinion</em> instead (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>). <em>Personal</em> adds nothing: every opinion is personal. (4 words become 3.)</li>\n<li><em>She has a tendency to become vengeful when opposed.</em> Use <em>She becomes vengeful when opposed</em> (<em>vengeful</em> = wanting revenge). “Five of them are replaced with just one”: <em>has a tendency to become</em> (5 words) becomes <em>becomes</em> (1 word) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>I came to the realisation that the office remains open till eight in the night.</em> Use <em>I realised that …</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>). <em>Came to the realisation that</em> (5 words) becomes <em>realised that</em> (2 words).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The exercise the lecture sets:</strong> write something yourself, find the phrases that add no meaning, and cut each one down to one or two words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Clichés</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s list (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>): <em>Make no mistake</em> · <em>It is not rocket science</em> · <em>Agree to disagree</em> (also <em>I beg to disagree</em>) · <em>It is what it is</em> · <em>Leave no stone unturned</em> · <em>Last but not (the) least</em>.</p>\n<p>They “do not add any meaning”. They are so overused that they become <strong>trite</strong> (dull from overuse), boring and repetitive (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Jargon</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Every discipline, and every sub-field, has its own jargon: words that specialists use among themselves (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>). The lecture links this to <strong>discourse conventions</strong>: the guidelines, methods and keywords that each field follows (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>). There is also <strong>general jargon</strong> that people use casually. The slide’s list (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>): <em>tip of the iceberg</em>, <em>low-hanging fruit</em>, <em>bottom line</em>, <em>big picture</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why avoid even general jargon? It does not “contribute to the meaning”, and its meanings are <strong>metaphorical</strong>, not literal. So many readers “may not relate to what has been said”, and the result is confusion and ambiguity (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Plain words for clichés and jargon (mine)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cliché or jargon</th>\n<th>Plain words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>leave no stone unturned</em></td>\n<td><em>check every possibility</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>last but not least</em></td>\n<td><em>finally</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>it is not rocket science</em></td>\n<td><em>it is simple</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>tip of the iceberg</em></td>\n<td><em>a small part of a larger problem</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>low-hanging fruit</em></td>\n<td><em>the easiest tasks</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>the bottom line</em></td>\n<td><em>the main point</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>the big picture</em></td>\n<td><em>the overall situation</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"how-to-avoid-cliche-s-and-jargon\">How to avoid clichés and jargon</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clichés</strong> creep in when you are not concentrating, or when you have written for too long. The brain is “overworked” and “refusing to think about anything new” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>). <strong>Take a break</strong>, then look at your work afresh.</li>\n<li><strong>Jargon</strong>: “sit back, relax and think of new analogies”, new comparisons, in place of the jargon (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>How to spot them</strong>: notice where your eye <strong>skips</strong> a phrase while you read your work. Your brain “is purposefully avoiding it”, most often because it is a cliché or jargon (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"objective-tone\">Objective tone</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Objective tone</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The tone of formal writing: <strong>objective</strong> (based on facts and evidence) and <strong>impersonal</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>; again in <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). The slide “How to use objective tone in professional writing?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>) gives five rules. Tone, style and clarity “feed into each other” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Put the emphasis on ideas and things, not on people and feelings</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Avoid: <em>I think that the argument is valid.</em></li>\n<li>Write: <em>These findings indicate that the arguments are valid.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The personal pronoun <em>I</em> goes, and the focus moves to <em>the findings</em>. That “sounds more formal and more professional” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Avoid evaluative words without evidence</strong>: “Evaluative words that are suggestive of non-evidence based findings or observations must be avoided” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>). (<em>Evaluative</em> = judging something good or bad.) If a judgement does not rest on evidence, do not make it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Avoid intense and emotional expressions</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>): both absolute praise and absolute blame. Make “more neutral choices” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Avoid</th>\n<th>Write instead</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>The writing is terrible.</em></td>\n<td><em>The writing is less convincing.</em> / <em>The writing needs improvement.</em> / <em>More work could be done on this.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>The writing is wonderful.</em> / <em>This is excellent.</em></td>\n<td><em>The writing is well argued.</em> / <em>well put.</em> / <em>well researched.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use modalities to allow room for disagreement</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>). A <strong>modality</strong> shows the attitude of the speaker or writer: obligation, possibility or ability (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>). (Modal verbs were in <a href=\"#/english1/w03/modal-verbs\">Week 3</a>.)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absolute: <em>The policy does not negatively affect any sections of the society.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>)</li>\n<li>With the modality of possibility: <em>There is a possibility that the policy works well for everyone.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The second sentence leaves “some room for disagreement”: a reader can still argue that the policy harms some section of society (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>). (The slide writes <em>works good</em>; the correct adverb is <em>well</em>. This note is mine.)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use authentic sources for credibility</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>). Refer to good books, authors, articles and research papers; quote them and cite them. Then the reader sees that the work is “well researched” and backed by evidence, and by established authors and organisations (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ex0qeyRwv-g&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>).</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"editing\">Editing</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.4 takes the last element: <strong>editing and proofreading</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). The first slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=10s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:10</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Editing and proofreading constitute the next significant steps after the <strong>first draft</strong> of a piece of writing.” (<em>Draft</em> = an early version.)</li>\n<li>“Both include steps towards the revision of the written work for its content, style, language, and format.”</li>\n<li>“Editing involves revision of <strong>logistic</strong> errors in writing.”</li>\n<li>“Proofreading rectifies the grammar and <strong>surface-level</strong> errors in writing.” (<em>Rectify</em> = correct.)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Editing and proofreading</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Editing</th>\n<th>Proofreading</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Looks at</td>\n<td>the <strong>organisation</strong> of the writing: its “logistic aspects”</td>\n<td><strong>surface-level</strong> errors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Covers</td>\n<td>content, overall structure, clarity, style</td>\n<td>spelling, grammar, punctuation, format</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Here <em>logistic</em> means organisational: how the parts are arranged and work together (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Editing revises <strong>four aspects</strong> of your writing (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=50s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:50</a>): <strong>content</strong>, <strong>overall structure</strong>, <strong>clarity</strong> and <strong>style</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"editing-for-content\">Editing for content</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>) asks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is the <strong>major question</strong> answered? (The lecturer writes <em>topic</em> next to it.) <strong>Or</strong>: is the <strong>purpose</strong> stated clearly?</li>\n<li>Do the <strong>supporting statements</strong> engage the purpose statement or the topic sentence? First check the topic sentence; then check that the supporting statements address it “effectively” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"editing-for-overall-structure\">Editing for overall structure</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:10</a>) asks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is there a clear <strong>introduction</strong> and <strong>conclusion</strong>? For a paragraph, these are the topic sentence and the concluding sentence (“concluding remarks”) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Is the <strong>order</strong> of the sentences logical? This is <strong>cohesion</strong>: “you have to ensure that there is enough unity and cohesion in your paragraph” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). (<em>Cohesion</em> = sticking together.) Does the topic sentence lead “organically” (naturally) to the supporting sentences? Are the supporting sentences connected to each other, so that they build the main idea (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>)?</li>\n<li>Are the <strong>transitions</strong> between sentences and paragraphs clearly visible? In a longer essay, check the flow between paragraphs too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"editing-for-clarity\">Editing for clarity</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=340s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:40</a>) asks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Are all the <strong>terms clearly defined</strong>? Check each word and phrase: does it carry the meaning you want (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)? Avoid jargon; use words that your target audience can access (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Do the sentences carry the <strong>meaning the author intended</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)?</li>\n<li>Does the <strong>choice of words</strong> express the ideas effectively? The slide’s checklist: “free from vagueness, non-wordy, non-clichéd, non-ornate etc.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The word-choice checklist (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Ask yourself</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Free from vagueness</td>\n<td>Any vague or ambiguous expressions? (<em>Vague</em> = not clear or exact.)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-wordy</td>\n<td>Any “wordy lengthy breathless sentences”?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-clichéd</td>\n<td>Any expression that is trite, overused, “ineffective and boring”? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-ornate</td>\n<td>Any “overly decorated, embellished” statements? Any <strong>archaic</strong> or <strong>obsolete</strong> (“old, out fashioned”) expressions? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<em>Ornate</em> = too decorated. <em>Archaic</em> = very old, no longer in ordinary use. <em>Obsolete</em> = no longer used.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"editing-for-style\">Editing for style</h3>\n<p>Formal style has its own features: the language is <strong>direct</strong> and <strong>straightforward</strong>, and the tone is <strong>impersonal</strong> and <strong>objective</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=520s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:40</a>) asks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is the <strong>tone</strong> appropriate for the audience (objective and impersonal)? Know your target audience first (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). Could the tone be ironic, intellectual or emotional? “Emotional tone, however, does not have a space in formal writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). For an objective, impersonal tone, check that the work is free from:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>bias</strong>: gender, racial and ethnic bias, and bias about disability, age and sexual orientation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>);</li>\n<li><strong>jargon</strong>: technical jargon and in-group language, “group jargon”;</li>\n<li><strong>personal pronouns</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>contractions</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Are there any <strong>unnecessary phrases</strong>, such as <em>due to the fact that</em>, where you could simply write <em>because</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>)?</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The editing checklist</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Ask</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Content</td>\n<td>Is the question answered, and the purpose clear? Do the supporting sentences engage the topic sentence?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Structure</td>\n<td>A clear introduction and conclusion? A logical order? Visible transitions?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarity</td>\n<td>Terms defined? The meaning intended? Words free from vagueness, wordiness, cliché and ornament?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Style</td>\n<td>Is the tone objective and impersonal for this audience? Any unnecessary phrases?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"proofreading\">Proofreading</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Proofreading</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Revision or correction of errors at the surface levels”: spelling, grammar, punctuation “and so on” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"four-proofreading-techniques\">Four proofreading techniques</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=700s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:40</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Read your work aloud</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). Reading aloud catches errors that “slipped your sight” while you read silently (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Make a list of errors that you commonly commit, and look for them as a first step</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Proofread for only one error at a time</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). Check the whole document for spelling; then take it again for punctuation; and so on. “It is a tedious process; however, it ensures that all the errors ... are corrected properly” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). (<em>Tedious</em> = long and boring.)</li>\n<li><strong>Proofreading also checks the format of the writing</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). Different kinds of writing follow different templates and formats.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"what-proofreading-checks\">What proofreading checks</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>) lists what proofreading corrects: punctuation; citations; page numbers; headers and footers; proper names; fonts (type and size); indentation; spacing (between the sentences and between sections); alignment; spelling; grammar.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Punctuation</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>): did you use the Oxford (series) comma? The semicolon? Is the first-person pronoun <em>I</em> a capital letter?</li>\n<li><strong>Format</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>): reports, papers and proposals, above all for publication, need citations, page numbers, an index, indentation, headers, footers, fonts and alignment. An outside agency or organisation often sets rules for the font type and size, the indentation and the spacing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Spelling and grammar</strong>: “the major part of your proofreading” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(A <em>citation</em> names the source of a quotation or a fact. A <em>header</em> and a <em>footer</em> are the lines at the top and at the bottom of every page. <em>Indentation</em> is the space at the start of a line. <em>Alignment</em> is how the lines line up: left, right or centre. A <em>proper name</em> is the name of one particular person, place or organisation, which must be spelt exactly.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-fourteen-most-common-surface-errors\">The fourteen most common surface errors</h3>\n<p>The three main areas of surface errors are <strong>spelling</strong>, <strong>grammar</strong> and <strong>punctuation</strong>; formatting is a fourth (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). The slides (from <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1030s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:10</a>) list fourteen errors (the sorting into areas is mine):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>No.</th>\n<th>Error</th>\n<th>Area</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Missing comma after an introductory phrase</td>\n<td>punctuation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Vague pronoun reference</td>\n<td>grammar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Missing comma in a compound sentence</td>\n<td>punctuation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Wrong words (<em>advice</em>, <em>advise</em>)</td>\n<td>spelling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Wrong or missing verb endings</td>\n<td>grammar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Comma splices or fused sentences</td>\n<td>punctuation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Missing or misplaced possessive apostrophes</td>\n<td>punctuation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Unnecessary shifts in tense</td>\n<td>grammar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Wrong shifts in pronouns</td>\n<td>grammar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Sentence fragments</td>\n<td>grammar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>No agreement between subject and verb</td>\n<td>grammar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Missing commas in a series</td>\n<td>punctuation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>No agreement between pronouns and antecedents</td>\n<td>grammar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Dangling or misplaced modifiers</td>\n<td>grammar</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">1. Missing comma after an introductory phrase (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>According to the secretary, the files should be submitted in a sealed envelope.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>According to the secretary</em> is an <strong>introductory phrase</strong>: it comes before the main sentence.</li>\n<li>Without it, <em>The files should be submitted in a sealed envelope</em> is “a perfectly grammatical sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</li>\n<li>But writing is not only grammar; it is also <strong>impact</strong> on the reader. Such phrases build a link between sentences, like the transitional devices of 12.2 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). Concluding phrases (<em>I would like to conclude that</em>, <em>in conclusion</em>, <em>to summarize</em>) do the same job (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Rule:</strong> separate any such linking phrase from the rest of the sentence with a <strong>comma</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">2. Vague pronoun references (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>She gets a bus first and then the bike to go to her school. It is blue in colour.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>She gets a bus first and then the bike to go to her school. The bus is blue in colour.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>A <strong>pronoun</strong> (<em>it, she, they</em> …) stands in place of a noun (<a href=\"#/english1/w02/pronouns\">Week 2</a>).</li>\n<li>Here <em>it</em> could mean the bus or the bike. We cannot tell which one is blue (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Rule:</strong> when the pronoun could point to more than one noun, repeat the noun instead. <em>The bus</em> or <em>the bike</em>: either is fine, as long as it is specific. Specificity is “a very important feature of professional writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">3. Missing comma in a compound sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>I will write the email, and I will send the report to the manager.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>A <strong>compound sentence</strong> is two independent clauses joined by a <strong>coordinating conjunction</strong> (<em>for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so</em>).</li>\n<li>Here: <em>I will write the email</em> + <em>and</em> + <em>I will send the report to the manager</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rule:</strong> put a comma after the first clause, <strong>before</strong> the coordinating conjunction (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">4. Wrong words: advice or advise? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>You should all take the advise seriously.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>You should all take the advice seriously.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>In this sentence the word is a <strong>noun</strong>: a thing you take (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The noun is spelt with <strong>-ce</strong>: <em>advice</em>. The <strong>verb</strong> is spelt with <strong>-se</strong>: <em>advise</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n<li>So <em>the advise</em> is a <strong>spelling</strong> error (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Hear the difference</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Advice</em> ends in an /s/ sound, like <em>ice</em>; <em>advise</em> ends in a /z/ sound, like <em>wise</em>. <em>I advise you to take my advice.</em> (This tip is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">5. Wrong or missing verb endings (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>The new staff member always leaves her desk unattended.</em> · <em>The new staff member decided to work promptly on the files.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <em>-s</em> of <em>leaves</em> and the <em>-ed</em> of <em>decided</em> are <strong>grammatical suffixes</strong> (endings) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The <em>-s</em> marks the <strong>present</strong> tense (after <em>he</em>, <em>she</em>, <em>it</em> or a singular noun); the <em>-ed</em> marks the <strong>past</strong> tense.</li>\n<li>Drop them (<em>The new staff member always leave …</em>, my wrong example) and “your sentence can become ungrammatical” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">6. Comma splices or fused sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>I am skilled at typing, I can type at a high speed.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Two independent sentences are joined by only a comma, with no conjunction. This “fusing” gives the reader no “heads up”, and it is not standard English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>). (<em>Splice</em> = join two ends together.)</li>\n<li><strong>Fix 1, a semicolon:</strong> <em>I am skilled at typing; I can type at a high speed.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>)</li>\n<li><strong>Fix 2, a comma and a coordinating conjunction:</strong> <em>I am skilled at typing, and I can type at a high speed.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>)</li>\n<li><strong>Fix 3, a full stop:</strong> <em>I am skilled at typing. I can type at a high speed.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">7. Missing or misplaced possessive apostrophes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Missing:</strong> <em>Ashas office desk looks really neat.</em> <strong>Right:</strong> <em>Asha’s office desk looks really neat.</em> The desk <strong>belongs</strong> to Asha, so it needs <em>'s</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Misplaced:</strong> <em>Officer’s should report to the centre on time.</em> <strong>Right:</strong> <em>Officers should report to the centre on time.</em> Nothing belongs to anyone here; <em>officers</em> is just a <strong>plural</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Plural possessive</strong>: for something that belongs to many officers, write <em>officers’</em>, with the apostrophe after the <em>s</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>belongs to one person</td>\n<td><em>Asha’s desk</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>more than one</td>\n<td><em>officers</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>belongs to many</td>\n<td><em>the officers’ room</em> (my example)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">8. Unnecessary shifts in tense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>The reports are presented to the clients, they have been verified.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>The reports are presented to the clients; they are verified.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Are presented</em> is in the simple present; <em>have been verified</em> shifts to the present perfect (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</li>\n<li>If you begin in the present tense, stay in it to the end; if you begin in the past, stay in the past. This matters most in long documents such as reports and proposals (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“If the context does not expect you or does not demand you to change the tense, then do not do it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The right sentence also changes the comma to a <strong>semicolon</strong>, which mends a comma splice (error 6). (This observation is mine.)</li>\n<li>A caution (mine): <em>have been verified</em> is still a <strong>present</strong> form, and the present perfect even fits the meaning here (the checking came before the presenting), so with a semicolon <em>The reports are presented to the clients; they have been verified</em> is also correct. A plainer needless shift is from present to past: ✗ <em>She opens the file and read the report.</em> ✓ <em>She opens the file and reads the report.</em></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">9. Wrong shifts in pronouns (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>He was on their way back from work.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>He was on his way back from work.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>He</em> is <strong>singular</strong> in number and <strong>masculine</strong> in gender (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</li>\n<li>A later pronoun for the same person must match both. So <em>his</em>: not <em>their</em> (plural), and not <em>her</em> (feminine) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">10. Sentence fragments (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>fragment</strong> is a piece of a sentence written as if it were a whole one. Often the <strong>subject</strong> is missing, and “you cannot have a sentence without that” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>Want to attend the meeting.</em> There is no subject. <strong>Right:</strong> <em>I want to attend the meeting.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>)</li>\n<li><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>Since yesterday, the work has been postponed, waiting for it to resume.</em> The part <em>waiting for it to resume</em> has no subject of its own. <strong>Right:</strong> <em>The work has been postponed since yesterday. I am waiting for it to resume.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Run-on or fragment?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture says “run-on sentences or fragments” together (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>). Most grammar books keep them apart. A <strong>fragment</strong> is an incomplete sentence (error 10). A <strong>run-on</strong> is two sentences run together, like the fused sentence of error 6. (This note is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">11. No agreement between subject and verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>I likes the ambience of my office.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>I like the ambience of my office.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The subject <em>I</em> is in the first person. With <em>I</em>, a present-tense verb takes <strong>no -s</strong>: <em>I like</em>. Only <em>he</em>, <em>she</em>, <em>it</em> and singular nouns take <em>likes</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>). (<em>Ambience</em> = the feel or mood of a place.)</li>\n<li>Agreement is “a very, very, very important feature in all English sentences” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>). See <a href=\"#/english1/w05/subject-verb-agreement\">Week 5</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">12. Missing commas in a series (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>The reports of the previous meeting, files and writing pads must be laid on the table before the members of the board arrive here.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>The reports of the previous meeting, files, and writing pads must be laid on the table before the members of the board arrive here.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The list has three items: <em>the reports of the previous meeting</em>, <em>files</em>, <em>writing pads</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Rule:</strong> “When you have more than two items in a list, they must be separated using a comma”, and that includes a comma before <em>and</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>).</li>\n<li>This comma before <em>and</em> is the <strong>series comma</strong>, also called the <strong>Oxford comma</strong>.</li>\n<li>A caution (mine): the commas <strong>between</strong> the items are always needed, but the comma before <em>and</em> is a choice of style. Many British and Indian publishers leave it out, so <em>meeting, files and writing pads</em> is not wrong everywhere. Follow the lecture, or the style your organisation uses, and use it the same way throughout.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">13. No agreement between pronouns and antecedents (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>Each of the emails have been sent to their recipients.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>Each of the emails has been sent to its recipients.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>antecedent</strong> of a pronoun is “the reference point of the pronoun”: the noun that the pronoun refers back to (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Here the pronoun <em>their</em> refers to <em>each of the emails</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2100s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Each of the emails</em> is <strong>singular</strong>: it means every single email, one at a time, “among the many” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:30</a>).</li>\n<li>So the pronoun must be singular: <em>their</em> becomes <em>its</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>).</li>\n<li>And the verb must be singular: <em>have</em> becomes <em>has</em>. That makes two changes, both for singularity (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">14. Dangling or misplaced modifiers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Wrong:</strong> <em>I have submitted the paper clips to the department that were left over.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Right:</strong> <em>I have submitted the paper clips that were left over to the department.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>A <strong>modifier</strong> is a word or phrase that describes another part of the sentence. A modifier should always stand next to what it modifies; the two cannot be separated (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The modifier here is <em>that were left over</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:30</a>).</li>\n<li>What was left over? Not the department: <strong>the paper clips</strong>.</li>\n<li>So place the modifier right after <em>the paper clips</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Your own list first</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>These fourteen are “the most commonly committed errors”, and your own list may be longer. Make a list of <strong>your</strong> errors, check for those first, and then check for these fourteen (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2310s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=po55EcHnXbY&amp;t=2340s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 39:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"writing-for-an-exam\">Writing for an exam</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.5, by Karthika Sathyanathan: how to improve your writing for <strong>academic writing exams</strong>, such as <strong>IELTS</strong> (the test of Lecture 7.5, <a href=\"#/english1/w07\">Week 7</a>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). Its strategies help you “perform in your academic writing tests pretty decently” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"keep-it-simple\">Keep it simple</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Keep it simple</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=40s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:40</a>): “Simple done accurately is better than complicated done inaccurately. It is better to keep things simple and do it well rather than overcomplicate everything and do it badly. Show off your accurate English. Not the English that you are not sure of.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"set-phrases-and-linking-words\">Set phrases and linking words</h3>\n<p><strong>Prepare set phrases and keywords</strong> that you can use in the exam (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). That does not mean memorising lists and reproducing them: “Do not rehearse what you are going to write.” But keep “a collection of phrases that you could possibly use if the time arises”, “and it definitely would arise” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). The lecturer made such a list for her own exam, “and it really did help me” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Have linking words ready</strong> (the slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=40s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:40</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:50</a>; spoken at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>To …</th>\n<th>Linking words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>compare</td>\n<td><em>in the same way, at the same time</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>contrast</td>\n<td><em>on the contrary, however</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>give examples</td>\n<td><em>for instance, to illustrate</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>give your opinion</td>\n<td><em>from my perspective, from my point of view</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>generalise</td>\n<td><em>overall, all things considered</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>express certainty</td>\n<td><em>undoubtedly, of course</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>express partial agreement</td>\n<td><em>in a way, to some extent</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>show cause</td>\n<td><em>owing to, due to</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>show effect</td>\n<td><em>for this reason, consequently</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mark time</td>\n<td><em>to begin with, following this</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>add information</td>\n<td><em>furthermore, moreover</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>express condition</td>\n<td><em>unless, provided that</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>conclude</td>\n<td><em>in summary, in short</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Learn each word with its job</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Overall</em> and <em>all things considered</em> <strong>generalise</strong> (they give the general picture); <em>undoubtedly</em> and <em>of course</em> express <strong>certainty</strong>. <em>Owing to</em> and <em>due to</em> show a <strong>cause</strong>; <em>for this reason</em> and <em>consequently</em> show an <strong>effect</strong>. Do not mix them up. (The pairing is mine; the lists are the lecture’s.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The same family as 12.2</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>These are the <strong>transitional devices</strong> of Lecture 12.2, with more jobs. <em>Furthermore</em> and <em>moreover</em> are in both lists, to add. (This link is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"formal-and-short\">Formal and short</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No contractions</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). Writing tests are “a serious affair”, so they are formal writing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). Write <em>cannot</em>, not <em>can’t</em>; <em>do not</em>, not <em>don’t</em>; <em>shall not</em>, not <em>shan’t</em>; <em>I am</em>, not <em>I’m</em>; <em>going to</em>, not <em>gonna</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reduce your word count</strong>: “use as few words as possible” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). There are three ways (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:50</a>):\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Avoid deadweight words</strong>: <em>very, so, really, a lot</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Replace empty words with strong words</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>): <em>very, very happy</em> or <em>really, really happy</em> becomes <em>elated</em>; <em>so scared</em> becomes <em>terrified</em>. (<em>Elated</em> = extremely happy. <em>Terrified</em> = extremely afraid.)</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid <em>there is</em> and <em>there are</em></strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Count the words saved (the lecture’s sentence at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>There are many problems that we may encounter</em>: There (1) are (2) many (3) problems (4) that (5) we (6) may (7) encounter (8). That is <strong>8 words</strong>. (<em>Encounter</em> = meet, face.)</li>\n<li><em>We may encounter many problems</em>: We (1) may (2) encounter (3) many (4) problems (5). That is <strong>5 words</strong>.</li>\n<li>Words saved: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span></span>,</span> and the meaning is the same.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the second sentence is 3 words shorter. “Why write unnecessary words?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"read-check-and-get-it-checked\">Read, check, and get it checked</h3>\n<p>The last slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:20</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Read the right documents</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). What does the exam ask you to write: articles, reports, essays? Read those kinds of writing. “If they are expecting you to do a notice writing in the exam, then there is no point in learning the format and phrases and keywords required for letter writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Check your work</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>): “If you don’t check them, you don’t see them”: if you do not check for errors, you do not see them. “Write less and check more; fewer words, higher accuracy” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). A writing exam expects <strong>perfection</strong>, so a short, precise, accurate essay beats a lengthy one.</li>\n<li><strong>Write, and get it checked</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). While you prepare, after you check your own work, ask someone who writes better than you to correct it: a teacher, a friend, a sibling, a senior, someone with experience of proofreading. Their inputs “can definitely help you improve your writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SP97dPNQmLM&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The exam routine</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>When</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>While you prepare</td>\n<td>read the kinds of writing the exam asks for; collect set phrases and linking words; get your practice work checked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>While you write</td>\n<td>keep it simple and accurate; no contractions; no deadweight words; strong words; no <em>there is</em> or <em>there are</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At the end</td>\n<td>check: write less, check more</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(The table is mine, built from the lecture.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Professional writing</strong> (12.1): authentic information, by systematic rules, for a specific audience, with a purpose (catalogues, brochures, a tablet strip, user manuals, workplace reports and emails). Literary writing is creative, written to entertain, figurative, emotional and for general readers. Professional writing manages information so that readers can act; it informs and instructs; its language is direct, factual, specific and straightforward, with technical vocabulary, simple sentences and an impersonal, objective tone. Four elements: developed and organised paragraphs; an appropriate (formal) style; clarity of ideas; spelling and grammar.</p>\n<p><strong>Paragraphs</strong> (12.2): <strong>topic sentence</strong> (a claim that is arguable, reveals the purpose and connects to the rest; a complex sentence, a question or a pivot; first or second in professional writing) + <strong>supporting sentences</strong> (reasons; facts, data, statistics; quotations; examples) + <strong>concluding sentence</strong> (required in a stand-alone paragraph; restates or reinforces: a prediction, suggestion, recommendation, summary or final observation). <strong>Unity</strong> = one idea; <strong>coherence</strong> = logical continuity. Unify with logical continuity, repeated key words and transitional devices (add, contrast, conclude). Argue with an example, a story, compare and contrast (subtle differences only), cause and effect, data, or a definition; real paragraphs embed several patterns.</p>\n<p><strong>Formality and clarity</strong> (12.3): formal = less personal, for professional or academic purposes, with an objective tone; formality is a range set by the situation (medium and domain), audience expectations and purpose. Informal: group jargon; slang, idioms, colloquialisms, local words; stereotypical and biased language; <em>I</em>, <em>we</em>, <em>you</em>; contractions. Clarity: no wordy phrases, clichés or jargon. To avoid them, take a break, find new analogies, and notice what your eye skips.</p>\n<p><strong>Objective tone</strong> (12.3): ideas, not people; no evaluative words without evidence; no intense or emotional words (<em>less convincing</em>, <em>well argued</em>); modality, to leave room for disagreement; authentic sources.</p>\n<p><strong>Editing</strong> (12.4): the logistic (organisational) revision of content, overall structure, clarity and style. <strong>Proofreading</strong>: the surface (spelling, grammar, punctuation, format). Read aloud; check your own error list first; one error at a time; check the format. The 14 errors: the comma after an introductory phrase; vague pronouns; the comma in a compound sentence; <em>advice</em> and <em>advise</em>; verb endings; comma splices; apostrophes; tense shifts; pronoun shifts; fragments; subject–verb agreement; the series (Oxford) comma; pronoun–antecedent agreement; misplaced modifiers.</p>\n<p><strong>Exams</strong> (12.5): keep it simple and accurate; set phrases (not rehearsed answers) and linking words for 13 jobs; no contractions; fewer words (no deadweight, strong words, no <em>there is</em> or <em>there are</em>); read the right documents; write less, check more; get it checked.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Label each sentence of this paragraph as topic, supporting or concluding. Which kind of concluding sentence is it, and which strategies for unification does the paragraph use? <em>“Public libraries are still among the most useful places in a city. A library lends books, newspapers and journals free of cost. Many libraries also offer free internet and quiet rooms where students can study. Moreover, libraries run reading clubs for children during the holidays. In short, libraries offer free reading, free study space and activities for children.”</em> (The paragraph is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sentence 1</strong> is the <strong>topic sentence</strong>. It is a claim that someone could argue with, it declares the topic (public libraries) and its main point (they are useful), and it comes first, as professional writing recommends.</li>\n<li><strong>Sentences 2, 3 and 4</strong> are <strong>supporting sentences</strong>. They give reasons and examples: free books, free internet and study rooms, reading clubs.</li>\n<li><strong>Sentence 5</strong> is the <strong>concluding sentence</strong>. It <strong>summarises the key points</strong>. The paragraph stands alone, so the concluding sentence is required.</li>\n<li><strong>Unification:</strong> the key word <em>library</em> or <em>libraries</em> repeats in every sentence, and the transitional devices <em>also</em> and <em>moreover</em> (to add) and <em>in short</em> (to conclude) link the sentences. The paragraph keeps to one idea (unity), and each sentence leads on to the next (coherence).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Rewrite this sentence for a formal report, and name each problem you remove: <em>“I think the new canteen plan is awesome, and we’ve done a great job. Last but not least, it’s not rocket science that there are a lot of students who are really happy.”</em> (The sentence is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The problems, in order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>I think</em>: a first-person pronoun, and the emphasis is on a person, not on the idea (objective tone, rule 1).</li>\n<li><em>awesome</em>: slang, and an intense evaluative word.</li>\n<li><em>we’ve</em>: a contraction, and the first person <em>we</em>.</li>\n<li><em>done a great job</em>: an emotional judgement with no evidence (objective tone, rules 2 and 3).</li>\n<li><em>Last but not least</em>: a cliché.</li>\n<li><em>it’s</em>: a contraction.</li>\n<li><em>not rocket science</em>: a cliché.</li>\n<li><em>there are a lot of students who</em>: <em>there are</em> and the deadweight <em>a lot</em>.</li>\n<li><em>really happy</em>: the deadweight <em>really</em>; a neutral word fits a report better.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>A formal version:</strong> <em>Student feedback indicates that the new canteen plan is well received; many students are satisfied with it.</em> The emphasis is now on the feedback (the evidence), the judgement is neutral (<em>well received</em>), and every informal factor is gone.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name the surface error in each sentence, and correct it. (a) <em>According to the timetable the exam starts at nine.</em> (b) <em>Ravi met Arun at the station. He was tired.</em> (c) <em>Each of the reports have their own summary.</em> (d) <em>I finished the report, I sent it to the client.</em> (e) <em>The manager’s will meet on Monday.</em> (f) <em>She returned the books to the library that were damaged.</em> (The sentences are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Missing comma after an introductory phrase</strong> (error 1): <em>According to the timetable, the exam starts at nine.</em></li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Vague pronoun reference</strong> (error 2): <em>he</em> could be Ravi or Arun. Repeat the noun: <em>Ravi was tired.</em> (or <em>Arun was tired.</em>)</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Pronoun–antecedent and subject–verb agreement</strong> (errors 13 and 11): <em>each of the reports</em> is singular, so <em>Each of the reports has its own summary.</em> That is two changes: <em>have</em> to <em>has</em>, <em>their</em> to <em>its</em>.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Comma splice</strong> (error 6). Use any of three fixes: <em>I finished the report; I sent it to the client.</em> or <em>I finished the report, and I sent it to the client.</em> or <em>I finished the report. I sent it to the client.</em></li>\n<li>(e) <strong>Misplaced apostrophe</strong> (error 7): <em>managers</em> is a plural, and nothing belongs to anyone. <em>The managers will meet on Monday.</em></li>\n<li>(f) <strong>Misplaced modifier</strong> (error 14): the books were damaged, not the library. <em>She returned the books that were damaged to the library.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Editing or proofreading? For editing, name the aspect. (a) Checking that every paragraph keeps to one idea. (b) Checking that the page numbers and the font size follow a journal’s rules. (c) Replacing <em>due to the fact that</em> with <em>because</em>. (d) Reading the draft aloud to catch a missing word. (e) Asking whether the supporting sentences really prove the topic sentence. (f) Checking that no sentence uses <em>I</em> or a contraction.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Editing: overall structure.</strong> Unity and cohesion are part of the structure check.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Proofreading: format.</strong> Page numbers and fonts are surface features.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Editing: style.</strong> The style slide’s example of an unnecessary phrase is <em>due to the fact that</em>.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Proofreading.</strong> Reading aloud is its first technique.</li>\n<li>(e) <strong>Editing: content.</strong> Its question is whether the supporting statements engage the purpose statement or the topic sentence.</li>\n<li>(f) <strong>Editing: style.</strong> An objective, impersonal tone means no personal pronouns and no contractions.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Make each sentence objective, and name the rule you use. (a) <em>I believe the results are correct.</em> (b) <em>This proposal is excellent.</em> (c) <em>The new rule will never harm any student.</em> (d) <em>The report is terrible.</em> (e) <em>Everyone knows that reading improves memory.</em> (The sentences are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Ideas, not people</strong> (rule 1): <em>The data indicate that the results are correct.</em></li>\n<li>(b) <strong>No intense or emotional words</strong> (rule 3): <em>This proposal is well argued.</em> (or <em>well researched</em>)</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Use a modality</strong> (rule 4): <em>There is a possibility that the new rule works well for every student.</em> This leaves room for a reader to disagree.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>No intense or emotional words</strong> (rule 3): <em>The report needs improvement.</em> (or <em>is less convincing</em>)</li>\n<li>(e) <strong>No judgement without evidence, and authentic sources</strong> (rules 2 and 5), with a modality (rule 4): <em>Research on reading suggests that it may improve memory</em>, followed by a citation of that research. <em>Everyone knows</em> is a claim with no evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Exam writing. (a) Cut the word count of <em>There are a lot of students who are really, really scared of the exam.</em> Count the words at each step. (b) Give the job of each linking word: <em>owing to</em>, <em>provided that</em>, <em>to some extent</em>, <em>overall</em>, <em>following this</em>, <em>consequently</em>. (Sentence (a) is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a) Step by step:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The sentence as it is has <strong>14 words</strong>.</li>\n<li>Drop <em>There are … who</em> (avoid <em>there is</em> and <em>there are</em>): <em>A lot of students are really, really scared of the exam.</em> That leaves <strong>11 words</strong>.</li>\n<li>Replace the deadweight <em>a lot of</em> with <em>many</em>: <em>Many students are really, really scared of the exam.</em> That leaves <strong>9 words</strong>.</li>\n<li>Replace the empty <em>really, really scared</em> with the strong word <em>terrified</em>: <em>Many students are terrified of the exam.</em> That leaves <strong>7 words</strong>.</li>\n<li>Words saved: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">14</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span></span></span></span>,</span> which is half the sentence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(b) <em>Owing to</em>: show a <strong>cause</strong>. <em>Provided that</em>: express a <strong>condition</strong>. <em>To some extent</em>: express <strong>partial agreement</strong>. <em>Overall</em>: <strong>generalise</strong>. <em>Following this</em>: <strong>mark time</strong>. <em>Consequently</em>: show an <strong>effect</strong>.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
